lel to the forbidden direction \\((1,-1)\\) and contains exactly the \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=(i-1)x-(i-1)i+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,i) .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(i=1\\)*, \\(L_1\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n*If \\(i\\ge2\\)*, its slope \\(i-1\\) is an integer different from \\(0,-1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_n:=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)=P_a\\in L_a\\).\n* Otherwise \\(2\\le b\\le a\\le n-1\\).  In this case \\((a,b)=Q_{b}\\in L_{b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) is a valid configuration with **exactly one non‑sunny line** (the diagonal \\(D\\)) and **\\(n-1\\) sunny lines** (the lines \\(L_i\\) for \\(i\\ge2\\)).  Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1\n\\]\n\nis attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) we may replace any sunny line \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_i\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and contains the point \\(P_i=(i,1)\\) as well as all points \\((i,b)\\) of \\(S_n\\); in particular it still covers every point that was covered by \\(L_i\\).  Performing this replacement for exactly \\(t\\) of the sunny lines we obtain a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-1)-t\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Since \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis