ertical (non‑sunny) lines, without exceeding the point‑capacity bound proved in step 1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution (proved parts only)**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the forbidden slopes).  \n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then for an integer point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(\\ell\\) we must have  \n\\(x\\equiv x_0\\pmod q\\) for a fixed residue class \\(x_0\\).  Hence among the\n\\(n\\) possible integer abscissae \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) there are at most\n\\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) values that can give a lattice point of \\(\\ell\\).\nSince \\(q\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =M .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q=1\\) the slope is an integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  The line has equation\n\\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(b\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  The conditions\n\\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\) give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\nmx+b\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\n(1+m)x\\le n+1-b .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(m\\ge1\\) the last inequality forces\n\\(x\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nWhen \\(m\\le-2\\) we rewrite the second inequality as\n\\((1+m)x\\ge 1-b\\) and obtain the same bound.  In either case the number of\nadmissible integer \\(x\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nThus (2) holds for all sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) there is **no** family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.  By Lemma 1 each line\ncontains at most \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le nM .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, \\(M=n/2\\) and the right–hand side of (6) equals\n\\(n^{2}/2\\), whereas \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2>n^{2}/2\\); contradiction.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge2\\)).  Then \\(M=t+1\\) and\n\\(nM=(2t+1)(t+1)=t(2t+1)+t+1=|S_n|\\).  Equality in (6) would require that\nevery sunny line contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  The only line\nwith that many points is the diagonal \\(y=x\\) (slope 1); all other sunny\nlines contain at most \\(t\\) points.  Hence the total number of covered\npoints is strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\), again a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence of a covering with no sunny line)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the following \\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\) and\ncontain **no** sunny line:\n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\nV_i:\\;x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) then it lies on \\(D\\).\nOtherwise \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\nAll lines in (7) are parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the\nline \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence of a covering with all three lines sunny when \\(n=3\\))\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare all sunny, distinct, and together contain every point of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n\\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\nDirect substitution shows that each of these points satisfies the equation\nof at least one of the lines in (8).  The slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are\nnone of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all three lines are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Partial Conclusion\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (Lemma 3).  \n* For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable (Lemma 4).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Lemma 2).  \n\nThe construction of families with an **arbitrary** number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\)), remains to be found.  \nThe difficulty is to replace some of the non‑sunny vertical lines of\nLemma 3 by sunny lines while preserving the covering property and keeping\nthe total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  This part of the problem is\nstill open."
