lie on a\nsingle sunny line (a sunny line is never vertical).  Consequently each of the\n\\(n\\) lines contains **at most one** point of \\(C_1\\); because \\(|C_1|=n\\), every line\ncontains **exactly one** point of \\(C_1\\).\n\nNow look at the second column\n\\[\nC_2=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\nA line already used for a point of \\(C_1\\) can meet \\(C_2\\) in at most one further\npoint (otherwise the line would contain two points with the same abscissa, which is\nimpossible).  Hence the whole family can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C_2\\).\nBut \\(|C_2|=n-1\\); therefore at least one line does **not** meet \\(C_2\\) at all.\nSuch a line would then contain at most \\(L-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\), contradicting the\nnecessity that every line contains exactly \\(L\\) points.  The contradiction shows that\nno covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines exists for any odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\nThus we have proved Lemma\u202f2:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=n\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge4.\\;}\n\\]\n\n(The case \\(n=3\\) will be handled separately.)\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\nDefine the following set of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x - i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines*:\n\\[\nd:\\; x+y=n+1\\qquad\\text{(parallel to }x+y=0\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nv_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n-1-k)\\;+\\;1=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct: the vertical lines have different abscissae,\nthe sunny lines have different intercepts, and the diagonal is neither vertical\nnor of slope\u202f1.\n\nWe prove that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n(3)\u2013(4).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  Partition of the point set\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) set \\(\\Delta=b-a\\).\n\n*If \\(\\Delta\\in\\{0,-1,\\dots ,-(k-1)\\}\\)*, then \\(b-a=-i\\) for some\n\\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and the point belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*If \\(\\Delta\\notin\\{0,-1,\\dots ,-(k-1)\\}\\) and \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*,\nthe point lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).\n\n*If \\(\\Delta\\notin\\{0,-1,\\dots ,-(k-1)\\}\\) and \\(a\\le k\\)*,\nthen necessarily \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+1\\); consequently\n\\(a+b\\ge a+(a+1)=2a+1\\le 2k+1\\le n+1\\) (because \\(k\\le n-1\\)).\nHence the point satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) **iff** it is the unique point\n\\((n,1)\\); otherwise \\(a+b<n+1\\) and the point is already covered by a\nvertical line with abscissa \\(a\\ge k+1\\) \u2013 a contradiction.  The only point\nwith \\(a\\le k\\) that is not covered by a line \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\((n,1)\\), and it\nlies on the diagonal \\(d\\) because \\(n+1= n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (3)\u2013(4).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Count of sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); the other \\(n-k\\) lines are\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence the\nconstruction contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore Lemma\u202f3 holds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ and }0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ there exists a covering with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\nA direct verification shows that they contain respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n\\{(1,2),(2,3)\\},\\;\n\\{(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich together cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable for\n\\(n=3\\).  This is Lemma\u202f4.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final classification\n\nCollecting the results:\n\n* Lemma\u202f2 excludes \\(k=n\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n* Lemma\u202f3 provides a construction for **all** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n* Lemma\u202f4 shows that the remaining value \\(k=n\\) does occur when \\(n=3\\).\n\nConsequently, for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny\nlines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{n\\}\\ \\text{if }n=3\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\} &\\text{for every }n\\ge3,\\\\[2mm]\nk=n &\\text{only when }n=3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."

