            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### Notation  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of the integer lattice.\nLet \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)\\) be the primitive integer direction vector of \\(\\ell\\); i.e.  \n\\(\\gcd(|\\Delta a|,|\\Delta b|)=1\\) and \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)\\) points from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-coordinate changes when we move along the line, so \\(\\Delta b\\neq0\\); similarly \\(\\Delta a\\neq0\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b\\neq0\\).\n\nHence\n\\[\n|\\Delta a|+|\\Delta b|\\ge 2,\\qquad |\\Delta a+\\Delta b|\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nWhen we move from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next, the value of \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b\\); therefore the absolute change of \\(a+b\\) is at least \\(1\\).  \nConsequently, along \\(\\ell\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) can assume at most\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta a+\\Delta b|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=n .\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound is obtained by using the fact that \\(|\\Delta a|+|\\Delta b|\\ge 2\\).  \nSince the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has length \\(n\\), the number of distinct values of \\(a+b\\) that can appear on \\(\\ell\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|\\Delta a|+|\\Delta b|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) has a distinct value of \\(a+b\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le L:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nBy (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}|\\ell\\cap S_n|\n      \\le nL=n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, write \\(n=2m\\). Then \\(L=\\frac{n+1}{2}=m+\\tfrac12\\) and\n\\[\nnL=2m\\Bigl\\lfloor m+\\tfrac12\\Bigr\\rfloor=2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=\\frac{n^{2}}2\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\) (the case \\(n=3\\) will be treated\nseparately). Then \\(L=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2m+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and\n\\[\nnL=(2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\n<\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\]\n(The inequality is strict because \\(m\\ge2\\).)\n\nThus in every case \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(nL<|S_n|\\), contradicting the assumption that the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge4\\text{ a covering with }k=n\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (Covering with no sunny lines)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently it lies on the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\). Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered.  \n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is attainable for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4 (Covering with all sunny lines when \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all three\nlines are sunny. A straightforward verification shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n\\ell_2 &\\supset\\{(1,2),(2,3)\\},\\\\\n\\ell_3 &\\supset\\{(3,1)\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand the six points of \\(S_3\\) are exactly the union of these three sets. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3\\text{ is attainable for }n=3.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemmas 2, 3 and 4 we obtain the following rigorous statements:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of the set \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).\n* For \\(n=3\\) there exists a covering with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n=3\\)).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) a covering with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)) is impossible.\n\nThe existence of coverings for the intermediate numbers of sunny lines\n\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) has not been established in the present work.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
