            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le L:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)\\) be the primitive integer direction vector of \\(\\ell\\);\nbecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta a\\neq0,\\qquad \\Delta b\\neq0,\\qquad \\Delta a+\\Delta b\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nWhen we move from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next, the quantity\n\\(a+b\\) changes by \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b\\); hence \\(|\\Delta a+\\Delta b|\\ge1\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has\nlength \\(n\\).  Consequently the number of distinct values of \\(a+b\\) that can\nappear on \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|\\Delta a+\\Delta b|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\Delta a+\\Delta b|\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b=\\pm1\\) would\nforce \\(|\\Delta a|+|\\Delta b|=1\\), contradicting \\(\\Delta a,\\Delta b\\neq0\\)),\nthe right‑hand side does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf \\(n\\) is even then a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines is\nimpossible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor even \\(n\\) we have \\(L=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=n/2\\).\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, Lemma 1 gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le nL=n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}=\\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every even \\(n\\ge2\\), a\ncontradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k=0\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\)\nsatisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus the\n\\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd, then there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  Consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\nthey are sunny and they are distinct because the constants \\(c\\) differ.\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\) from (4.1).  Hence the \\(n\\)\nlines (4.1) cover every point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Inductive step)  \nLet \\(n\\ge4\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that for every integer \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m<n\\) and every\n\\(k'\\) obeying the analogue of (5.1) there exists a covering of \\(S_m\\) by\n\\(m\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny ones.  \nThen a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \nTake the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  Removing this line deletes the\nfirst column of points; after translating \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) the remaining\npoints form the set \\(S_{n-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis there is a\ncovering of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAdding back the line \\(x=1\\) yields a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines with\nstill exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n\\) and \\(n\\) odd.*  \nThis is precisely Lemma 4.\n\nThus in all admissible situations a required covering exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof  \n\nWe prove the statement of the problem by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nA direct inspection shows that the four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all\nrealizable (e.g. three vertical lines for \\(k=0\\); the three lines of\nLemma 4 for \\(k=3\\); and mixtures for \\(k=1,2\\)).  \n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the theorem true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge4\\).\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (5.1).  Lemma 5 provides a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the theorem holds for \\(n\\).\n\nTogether with Lemma 2 (which shows that the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible when\n\\(n\\) is even) we obtain the full description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\