                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, when \\(n\\) is odd every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and when \\(n\\) is even every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised (the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Maximum points of a sunny line).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write a primitive integer direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) as \\((p,q)\\) with \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \n   Then  \n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le L:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof idea.*  \n   The quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(p+q\\) when moving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.  \n   If \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) the bound follows immediately from the length \\(n\\) of the interval\n   \\([2,n+1]\\).  \n   If \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(|p|\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(p\\) or \\(q\\) would be \\(0\\)).  \n   Hence the \\(a\\)-coordinate jumps by at least \\(2\\) each step, so at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points can lie in the strip\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  Combining the two cases yields the claimed bound.\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)).**  \n   For even \\(n\\) we have \\(L=n/2\\).  If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, Lemma 1 would give  \n   \\(|S_n|\\le nL=n^{2}/2\\), but \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2>n^{2}/2\\).  Hence a covering with\n   \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (Covering with no sunny lines).**  \n   The vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\) (the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\)).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (Covering with all lines sunny for odd \\(n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  The \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\},\n   \\]\n   all have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny) and are distinct.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\).  Consequently these \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Lemma 5 (Inductive construction for the remaining values of \\(k\\)).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even},\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   - \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   - \\(k=3\\): Lemma 4 (here \\(m=1\\), lines \\(y=x-1,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+1\\)).  \n   - \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=3\\); a short check shows that every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on one of them.  \n   - \\(k=2\\): take the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\) together with the non‑sunny line \\(x=3\\); again each point of \\(S_3\\) is covered.  \n   Hence all four possibilities are realised.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for all integers \\(<n\\) (\\(n\\ge4\\)).\n\n   - **Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n     Use the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\).  Removing this line deletes the first column\n     \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) of points.  After translating \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) the\n     remaining points form exactly \\(S_{n-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis there is a covering of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Adding back the line \\(x=1\\) yields a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines with still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Case 2: \\(k=n\\) and \\(n\\) odd.**  \n     This is precisely Lemma 4.\n\n   Thus the inductive construction produces a covering for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\); Lemma 3, Lemma 4 and Lemma 5 exhibit constructions for all remaining values of \\(k\\).  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the Verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any sunny line \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le L:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((p,q)\\) be a primitive integer direction vector of \\(\\ell\\); thus\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would be\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(\\ell\\), then\n\\((a'-a,b'-b)=t(p,q)\\) for some non‑zero integer \\(t\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)=t(p+q).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has\nlength \\(n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).*  \nFrom (1) the values of \\(a+b\\) on \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) differ by at least \\(2\\).  Hence at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) distinct values can occur, and therefore\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Case 2: \\(|p+q|=1\\).*  \nBecause \\(p+q=\\pm1\\) while both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are non‑zero, we must have\n\\(|p|\\ge2\\) and \\(|q|\\ge1\\).  Hence the \\(a\\)-coordinate changes by at least\n\\(2\\) when moving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.  Since the\ncondition \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) allows at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) such\nsteps, we again obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBoth cases give the same bound, completing the proof. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf \\(n\\) is even then a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines does\nnot exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor even \\(n\\) we have \\(L=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=n/2\\).  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\).  By Lemma 1 each\nline contains at most \\(L\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le nL=n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}=\\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every even \\(n\\ge2\\), a\ncontradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k=0\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we\nhave \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines\ncover \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd, there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\nthey are sunny and they are distinct because the constants \\(c\\) differ.\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\), i.e. \\(b-a\\in[-m,m]\\).\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of exactly one line \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\)\nfrom (4.1).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines (4.1) cover every point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5  \nLet \\(n\\ge3\\).  For every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even},\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nThe four admissible values of \\(k\\) are realised as follows.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) (Lemma 3).  \n* \\(k=3\\): Lemma 4 with \\(m=1\\) gives the lines \\(y=x-1,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+1\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the non‑sunny lines\n  \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=3\\).  A direct inspection shows that each of the six points of\n  \\(S_3\\) lies on one of these three lines.  \n* \\(k=2\\): take the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\) together with the\n  non‑sunny line \\(x=3\\); again every point of \\(S_3\\) is covered.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for all integers \\(<n\\) (\\(n\\ge4\\)).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \nUse the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\).  Removing this line eliminates the column\n\\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) of points.  After the translation\n\\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) the remaining points constitute exactly \\(S_{n-1}\\).\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a covering of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) lines\nwith precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Adding back the line \\(x=1\\) yields a covering\nof \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines with still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n\\) and \\(n\\) odd.*  \nThis is precisely Lemma 4.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) satisfying (5.1) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists,\ncompleting the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, Lemma 4 provides a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, and\n  Lemma 5 together with Lemma 3 yields coverings for all \\(k\\) with\n  \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while Lemma 5\n  (with Lemma 3 as the base) gives coverings for every \\(k\\) with\n  \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
