ent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and each\ncovers the required points.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m-1\\ge 3\\); let\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) be a family of\n\\(m-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{m-1}\\).\nThe set \\(T_m\\) consists of \\(T_{m-1}\\) together with the new column\n\\(\\{(m,1)\\}\\).\n\nChoose a number \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) that is **not** the\n\\(y\\)-intercept of any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\) (there are at most\n\\(m-1\\) such intercepts, so such a \\(t\\) exists).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(m,1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-t}{m-1}\\); because \\(t\\neq 1\\) the\nslope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), and because \\(m\\ge 4\\) we have\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-t}{m-1}\\neq -1\\).  Hence \\(L_m\\) is sunny and\ndifferent from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\cup\\{L_m\\}\\) contains \\(m\\)\nsunny lines.  Every point of \\(T_{m-1}\\) is already covered, and the\nnew point \\((m,1)\\) lies on \\(L_m\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) covers all of\n\\(T_m\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\nTaking \\(m=n\\) in the lemma gives a configuration of \\(n\\) sunny lines\ncovering \\(T_n\\); therefore \\(k=n\\) is also attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  From the lemma to an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1.*  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Step 2.*  The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate the coordinates by the vector \\((-(n-k),0)\\) the set\n\\(T_k'\