any \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f1.*  Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   *Step\u202f2.*  The still uncovered points form the \u201csize\u2011\\(k\\) triangle\u201d\n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After translating by \\((-(n-k),0)\\) this set becomes the standard\n   triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f3 (All\u2011Sunny Lemma).*  \n   For every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\n   together contain all points of \\(\\widetilde T_m\\).  \n   The lemma is proved by induction on \\(m\\) (see the detailed solution).  \n\n   Applying the lemma with \\(m=k\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n   \\(\\widetilde T_k\\); translating back gives \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n   \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.*  The union of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines obtained in Step\u202f3 is a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on\n   at least one of them.\n\nSince the constructions work for every admissible \\(k\\) and we have shown\nthat \\(k=2\\) is impossible, the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is\n\\(\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary bounds on \\(k\\)\n\nThe column \\(\\{x=1\\}\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\nA sunny line meets this column in at most one of those points, whereas a\nvertical line (non\u2011sunny) can meet it in all of the