---------\n### 5.  An all\u2011sunny covering of a triangular lattice (All\u2011Sunny Lemma)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\forall m\\ge 3\\;\\exists\\;m\\text{ distinct sunny lines whose union\ncontains all points of }\n\\widetilde T_m:=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m,\\;1\\le b\\le m+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and together\ncontain the six points of \\(\\widetilde T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m-1\\ge 3\\); let\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) be a family of \\(m-1\\) sunny\nlines covering \\(\\widetilde T_{m-1}\\).\n\nThe set \\(\\widetilde T_m\\) consists of \\(\\widetilde T_{m-1}\\) together with\nthe new column  \n\n\\[\nC_m=\\{(m,1),(m,2),\\dots ,(m,m)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a number \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) that is **not** the\n\\(y\\)-intercept of any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\) (there are at most\n\\(m-1\\) such intercepts, so such a \\(t\\) exists).  Define  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\text{ the line through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(m,1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-t}{m-1}\\).  Because \\(t\\neq 1\\) the\nslope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); because \\(m\\ge 4\\) we have\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-t}{m-1}\\neq -1\\).  Hence \\(L_m\\) is sunny and is\ndifferent from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\cup\\{L_m\\}\\) contains \\(m\\) sunny\nlines.  Every point of \\(\\widetilde T_{m-1}\\) is already covered, and the\npoint \\((m,1)\\) lies on \\(L_m\\).  The remaining points of the column\n\\(C_m\\) are \\((m,2),\\dots ,(m,m)\\); each of them has abscissa \\(m\\) and\ntherefore is not on any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\).  However, for every\n\\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,m\\}