m \\(n+1\\).\n\nThus we have produced configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nStart from a configuration for \\(n-1\\) with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines\n(which exists by the induction hypothesis).  Such a configuration contains\nexactly two non‑sunny lines; we may assume they are\n\n* the horizontal line \\(H_1:y=1\\), and  \n* the diagonal \\(D_n:x+y=n\\) (used to cover the points with \\(a+b=n\\)).\n\nReplace the diagonal \\(D_n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; \\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1-1}{2-1}=n-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is\nsunny.  The point \\((1,1)\\) is already covered by \\(H_1\\); the point\n\\((2,n-1)\\) lies on the former diagonal \\(D_n\\), so after the replacement every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-1}\\) remains covered.  Now the configuration for\n\\(n-1\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nFinally add the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\) as in the inductive step.\nThe resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which\nare sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nLemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the constructions above\nshow that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can indeed be realised.\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step