s,\n      still exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones, and now covers \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).\n\n   2. **If \\(k=m\\) (the maximal possible value).**  \n      First cover the set \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) **entirely with sunny lines**.\n      This is possible because the inductive hypothesis guarantees a\n      configuration for \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with \\(m-1\\) sunny lines; we now replace\n      the single non\u2011sunny line in that configuration by a sunny line.\n      The replacement is performed as follows.\n\n      In the configuration for \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines\n      there is a non\u2011sunny line parallel to the diagonal, namely\n      \\(D_{m+1}:x+y=m+1\\).  Replace it by the sunny line  \n\n      \\[\n      L:\\; \\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,m-1),\n      \\qquad\\text{slope }m-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n      \\]\n\n      The new line still passes through \\((1,1)\\) (already covered by another\n      line) and through \\((2,m-1)\\), a point that belonged to the diagonal\n      \\(D_{m+1}\\); consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) remains covered.\n      All \\(m\\) lines are now sunny.\n\n      Finally add the diagonal \\(D_{m+2}\\) (non\u2011sunny) to cover the new\n      points with sum \\(m+2\\).  The total number of sunny lines is therefore\n      \\(m\\), i.e. \\(k=m\\).\n\n   In both sub\u2011cases we have produced a family of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines\n   covering \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) with exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of\n   sunny lines.\n\n   By induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1\u20132 give the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the inductive\n   construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be\n   attained.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three families  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b,\\qquad V_a:\\;x=a,\\qquad D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\qquad |D_n|=n,\n\\]\n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAny two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); a sunny line is not\nparallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\); if it contains two,\nthey are of the form \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets the line \\(x=1\\) in at most one\npoint; similarly it meets the line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point.  If it meets\nboth, the two points are exactly as described, and the slope equals\n\\(-\\dfrac{b-1}{a-1}\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nA sunny line that contains a point of \\(D_n\\) cannot contain any point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line passes through \\((i,n+1-i)\\in D_n\\) and also through\n\\((1,b)\\in S_n\\) (the case \\((a,1)\\) is symmetric).  Its slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-(n+1-i)}{1-i}\n   =\\frac{b-n-1+i}{1-i}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\le n\\), the numerator equals \\(-(n+1-i-b)\\).  The denominator is\n\\(1-i\\).  The quotient simplifies to \\(-1\\), i.e. the line would be parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence  \n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(D_n\\), and\nby Lemma\u202f3 a sunny line covering a point of \\(D_n\\) cannot cover any point of\n\\(S_n\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines together can cover at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(D_n\\) **and** at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Since \\(|S_n|=2n-1\\), at\nleast one point of \\(S_n\\) would remain uncovered unless at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines is non\u2011sunny.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of configurations for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) can be covered as follows.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): replace one of them by a sunny line, e.g. the line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\);\n* \\(k=2\\): two sunny lines, e.g. \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x-1\\), together with the\n  non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\);\n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines, e.g. \\(y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\).\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) there\nexists a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nLet \\(n=m+1\\) and let \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be given.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(k\\le m-1\\).*  \nTake a configuration for \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(which exists by the hypothesis).  Add the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny and covers precisely the new points\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\).  The resulting family has \\(n\\) lines, still\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny ones, and now covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(k=m\\) (the maximal possible value).*\n\n1. **Cover \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with only sunny lines.**  \n   By the inductive hypothesis there is a configuration \\(\\mathcal L'_m\\) of\n   \\(m\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.\n   In that configuration the unique non\u2011sunny line is the diagonal\n   \\(D_{m+1}:x+y=m+1\\).\n\n2. **Replace the diagonal by a sunny line.**  \n   Consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;\\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,m-1),\n   \\qquad\\text{slope }m-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L\\) is sunny.  It passes through \\((1,1)\\), a point already covered by\n   another line of \\(\\mathcal L'_m\\), and through \\((2,m-1)\\), a point that\n   belonged to the diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\).  Consequently every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) remains covered after we delete \\(D_{m+1}\\) and insert\n   \\(L\\).  All \\(m\\) lines are now sunny.\n\n3. **Add the new diagonal.**  \n   Finally add the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   It is non\u2011sunny and covers the points with sum \\(n+1\\).  The total number\n   of sunny lines is therefore \\(m=n-1\\), i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma\u202f3 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the inductive construction\nshows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can indeed be realised.\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
