\nclaim for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  All lines are distinct, the required points are\ncovered, and the indicated number of lines are sunny.  Hence the claim holds\nfor \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that the claim holds for some integer \\(m\\ge3\\); i.e. for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) there is a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Inductive step – construction for \\(n=m+1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le m+1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.  \\(k\\le m\\).**  \n\nTake a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) that realises the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n(the existence follows from the hypothesis).  \nAdd the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+2}:\\;x+y=m+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{m+2}\\) is parallel to the family \\(D_s\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\nIt contains precisely the new points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\setminus\\mathcal P_m .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{D_{m+2}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines, still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and\ncovers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.  \\(k=m+1\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n\nStart from a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) that already has \\(m\\) sunny lines.\nBy the inductive hypothesis such a family exists; in it the only non‑sunny\nline is the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+1 .\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(D_{m+1}\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;\\text{through }(1,m+1)\\text{ and }(2,m-1),\n\\qquad\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.\nBoth points \\((1,m+1)\\) and \\((2,m-1)\\) belong to the diagonal\n\\(D_{m+1}\\), so after the replacement every point of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) is still\ncovered, and