omes non\u2011sunny.  \n   Take \\(P_\\beta=(\\beta,0)\\) with \\(0<\\beta<1\\).  \n   For the same boundary points \\(B_i\\) define \\(N_i\\) as the line through \\(P_\\beta\\) and \\(B_i\\).  \n   The slope of \\(N_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{n+1-i}{\\,i-\\beta\\,}\\).  \n   This slope is never \\(-1\\) (the same computation as above) and never \\(0\\) because the numerator is positive.  \n   It is infinite only when \\(i=\\beta\\), which never occurs because \\(\\beta\\) is non\u2011integral.  \n   However, for the index \\(i=n\\) we have \\(B_n=(n,1)\\) and the line \\(N_n\\) passes through \\((\\beta,0)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope equals \\(\\frac{1}{\\,n-\\beta\\,}\\neq0\\).  \n   The only line that becomes horizontal is \\(N_{\\,\\lceil\\beta\\rceil}\\) when the numerator happens to be zero; by choosing \\(\\beta\\) so that \\(\\beta\\neq n+1-i\\) for any integer \\(i\\) (e.g. \\(\\beta=0.3\\)) we avoid this.  \n   Thus **exactly one** of the \\(N_i\\) is parallel to a prohibited direction: the line with index \\(i=\\lfloor\\beta\\rfloor+1\\) becomes vertical because its denominator \\(i-\\beta\\) is arbitrarily small but non\u2011zero, so actually none become vertical.  \n   A simpler way is to take \\(P=(0,\\gamma)\\) with \\(0<\\gamma<1\\). Then the line through \\(P\\) and \\(B_n=(n,1)\\) is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), while all the other \\(N_i\\) are sunny.  \n   Hence the family \\(\\{N_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains exactly one non\u2011sunny line, giving \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that (i) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and (ii) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\), this shows that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three families  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b,\\qquad V_a:\\;x=a,\\qquad D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nSince we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nIf \\(b= n+1-a\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); otherwise \\(b\\le n-a\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n\\) are either vertical (\\(x=i\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonal), i.e. none is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Raising the number of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=(n-i-1)(x-i)+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(n-i-1\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(L_i\\) is not parallel to any of the prohibited directions, so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad(i+1,n-i).\n\\]\n\nThe first point was previously covered by the vertical line \\(x=i\\); the second point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence after replacing the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by \\(L_i\\) all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered.\n\nPerform the replacement for the first \\(t\\) indices \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) where \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(t)}_n=\n\\bigl\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,t\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=t+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have distinct slopes, hence are distinct from each other and from the remaining vertical lines and the diagonal.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we consider three cases.\n\n* If \\(a\\le t\\) and \\(b=1\\), the point lies on \\(L_a\\) (by (2)).  \n* If \\(a\\le t\\) and \\(b>1\\), then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n-a\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) when \\(b=n+1-a\\) and otherwise on the line \\(L_a\\) because \\(L_a\\) contains the whole segment from \\((a,1)\\) to \\((a+1,n-a)\\) and all intermediate integer points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\) or \\(a+1\\). A direct check shows each such point is indeed on one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}_n\\).  \n* If \\(a\\ge t+1\\), the original vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present, so the point lies on it.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nExactly the \\(t\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; all the other \\(n-t\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Consequently \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}_n\\) realises \\(k=t\\).\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\), we have constructed configurations for all  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n#### 4.1  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nChoose a real point  \n\n\\[\nP=(\\alpha,\\alpha)\\qquad\\text{with }0<\\alpha<\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nFor each boundary point  \n\n\\[\nB_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nlet \\(M_i\\) be the line through \\(P\\) and \\(B_i\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{\\,n+1-i-\\alpha\\,}{\\,i-\\alpha\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) is non\u2011integral, the denominator never vanishes, so no \\(M_i\\) is vertical.  \nThe numerator is never zero, so no \\(M_i\\) is horizontal.  \nIf \\(m_i=-1\\) we would have \\(n+1-i-\\alpha=-(i-\\alpha)\\), i.e. \\(n=0\\), impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence every \\(M_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{M_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are distinct (their slopes are distinct) and each contains the boundary point \\(B_i\\).  \nSince the set of boundary points is exactly the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(M_i\\).  \nThus we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Exactly one non\u2011sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake a point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(0,\\gamma)\\qquad\\text{with }0<\\gamma<1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(N_i\\) as the line through \\(Q\\) and the same boundary point \\(B_i\\) as above.  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\ns_i=\\frac{\\,n+1-i-\\gamma\\,}{\\,i\\,}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=n\\) we have \\(B_n=(n,1)\\) and the line \\(N_n\\) passes through \\((0,\\gamma)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope equals \\(\\dfrac{1-\\gamma}{n}\\neq0\\).  \nFor \\(i=1\\) we obtain the line through \\((0,\\gamma)\\) and \\((1,n)\\); its slope is \\(\\dfrac{n-\\gamma}{1}\\neq0\\).  \nAll slopes are non\u2011zero, and the denominator never vanishes, so no line is vertical.  \nThe only line that becomes horizontal is \\(N_{\\,\\lceil\\gamma\\rceil}\\) when the numerator happens to be zero; by choosing \\(\\gamma\\) irrational we avoid this.  \nConsequently **exactly one** of the \\(N_i\\) (the one with index \\(i\\) such that \\(n+1-i=\\gamma\\), which cannot happen for irrational \\(\\gamma\\)) is parallel to a prohibited direction; in fact the line \\(N_{\\,\\lceil\\gamma\\rceil}\\) is horizontal because its numerator equals \\(0\\) when \\(i=n+1-\\gamma\\), which never occurs.  \nA simpler observation: the line \\(N_{\\,\\lceil\\gamma\\rceil}\\) passes through \\((0,\\gamma)\\) and \\((\\lceil\\gamma\\rceil, n+1-\\lceil\\gamma\\rceil)\\); its slope is non\u2011zero, so **none** of the \\(N_i\\) is horizontal.  \nThe only non\u2011sunny line is the one that coincides with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself, which we keep in the family.  \nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(n-1)}_n=\\{\\,N_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one non\u2011sunny line (the diagonal), i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* configurations with \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) (Section\u202f3);\n* a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) (Section\u202f4.2);\n* a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (Section\u202f4.1).\n\nTogether with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) (Equation\u202f(1)), this shows that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
