f \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nNow consider the left‑most column \\(C_1\\). It contains exactly \\(n\\) points:\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\nAt most two of them can lie on \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\); therefore at least \\(n-2\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines. Since a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), the only way a non‑vertical, non‑horizontal line can meet the column \\(x=1\\) is by being the line \\(x=1\\) itself (vertical) or by being the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Both of these lines can contain **all** points of \\(C_1\\) simultaneously, but they are each a single line. Hence to cover the \\(n-2\\) points of \\(C_1\\) that are not on \\(\\ell_1\\cup\\ell_2\\) we would need at least \\(n-2\\) distinct lines, each of which would have to be vertical \\(x=1\\) (impossible, because vertical lines are unique) or diagonal (also unique). Thus we would need more than one copy of a line, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct.\n\nFormally, the only non‑sunny lines that intersect the column \\(x=1\\) are:\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\));\n* horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) (which intersect the column at a single point \\((1,b)\\)).\n\nA horizontal line can cover at most one point of \\(C_1\\). Since we have at most one vertical line and at most one diagonal line, the total number of points of \\(C_1\\) that can be covered by non‑sunny lines other than horizontals is at most \\(2\\). The remaining \\(n-2\\) points would each require a distinct horizontal line, giving at least \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines. Adding the two possible non‑horizontal non‑sunny lines and the two sunny lines would give at least \\((n-2)+2+2=n+2\\) distinct lines, exceeding the allowed number \\(n\\). This contradiction shows that a covering family cannot contain two sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore \\(a\\le n-1\\); the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n\\) are either vertical (\\(x=i\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonal). Both families are prohibited, so none of the lines is sunny. Thus the configuration contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nReplace the diagonal line in the previous family by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y = x-(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(1)}_n=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf \\(b=n+1-a\\) then \\(b = a-(n-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-a\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(+1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, while all the vertical lines are non‑sunny. Consequently the configuration contains exactly one sunny line, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 this is impossible; the proof of Lemma 1 already shows that any such family would require more than \\(n\\) distinct lines to