edundant: the point \\((1,1)\\) is now on \\(L_1\\), and the point \\((m,m)\\) (which exists only when \\(m\\ge 3\\)) lies on \\(L_2\\). Deleting these two redundant lines leaves exactly \\((m+2)-2 = m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(m+1\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 – a non‑sunny part.*  \nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that are **not** covered by the vertical lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we set \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\), the condition becomes  \n\n\\[\na'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set is exactly \\(T_k\\) (a right‑angled triangle of size \\(k\\)) translated by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal L_k\\) covering \\(T_k\\). Translating each of these lines by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover the untranslated set of points described above.\n\n*Step 3 – the whole family.*  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\mathcal L_1+(n-k,0),\\dots ,\\mathcal L_k+(n-k,0)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the last \\(k\\) are sunny. By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Extremal values\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the family \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and clearly covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=n\\): Lemma 1 provides directly a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a configuration with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]"
