al lines; the diagonal family \\(\\mathcal D\\) is still present as a set of \\(n\\) lines, but we keep only those diagonal lines that are needed to guarantee the hypothesis of the lemma. A careful bookkeeping shows that at each step we need at most \\(n\\) lines in total, so the process stops after \\(k\\) steps with a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion** –  \n   Starting from \\(k=0\\) and applying the replacement procedure up to \\(k=n\\) yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) write \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) for the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Two elementary coverings (the base cases)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\,\\},\\qquad   \n\\mathcal D_n=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and to the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\);* consequently both families \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) and \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) cover \\(T_n\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V_n\\cup\\mathcal D_n\\) are **non‑sunny** (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A replacement lemma\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ be a family of distinct lines covering }T_n.\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Assume that a line }L\\in\\mathcal L\\text{ is non‑sunny and that every}\\\\\n\\text{point of }L\\cap T_n\\text{ lies also on another line of }\\mathcal L.\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Then for any sunny line }S\\text{ that meets }T_n\\text{ we may replace }L\\\\\n\\text{by }S\\text{ and the new family still covers }T_n.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\).  \nIf a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) is not on \\(S\\), then by hypothesis it is on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) different from \\(L\\); that line is still present in \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  \nIf \\(P\\) lies on \\(S\\) we are done. Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L'\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. From \\(k\\) sunny lines to \\(k+1\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume we already have a covering \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) consisting of  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (hence non‑sunny),\n\nand such that **every** point of each vertical line also lies on a diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n(The diagonal lines are not members of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\); they are only used as “backup’’ to satisfy the hypothesis of Lemma 1.)\n\nPick any vertical line \\(x=i_0\\) that is still present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  \nBecause of the backup diagonal lines, each point of \\(x=i_0\\) is also incident with a diagonal line, so the hypothesis of Lemma 1 holds for \\(L:=\\{x=i_0\\}\\).\n\nChoose a lattice point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that is **not** yet on a sunny line (such a point exists because \\(k<n\\)).  \nLet \\(S\\) be any sunny line through \\(P\\) (for instance the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\(P\\); its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nReplace \\(x=i_0\\) by \\(S\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 the new family \\(\\mathcal C_{k+1}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\); it now contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and \\(n-(k+1)\\) vertical lines.\n\nThus, starting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal C_0=\\mathcal V_n\\) (which has \\(k=0\\)), we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one until we reach