n\\[\nP_j=(i_j,\\,y_j)\\in T_n,\n\\qquad\\text{where }1\\le y_j\\le n+1-i_j .\n\\]\n\nFor a given point \\(P_j\\) consider the (finite) set  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_j=\\Bigl\\{\\text{slope of }P_jQ\\;\\Big|\\;Q\\in T_n,\\;Q\\neq P_j\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery line through \\(P_j\\) that meets another point of \\(T_n\\) has a slope belonging to \\(\\Sigma_j\\).  \nThe three slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are also forbidden because they make the line non\u2011sunny.\n\nSince \\(\\Sigma_j\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\bigl(\\Sigma_j\\cup\\{0,-1\\}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nis infinite; choose any \\(\\alpha_j\\) from it.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-y_j=\\alpha_j\\,(x-i_j).\n\\]\n\nBy construction  \n\n* \\(\\alpha_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_j\\) is sunny;\n* \\(\\alpha_j\\notin\\Sigma_j\\); therefore \\(S_j\\) contains **no** point of \\(T_n\\) other than \\(P_j\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct, sunny, and each meets \\(T_n\\) in exactly one of the chosen points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Adding the remaining non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V'_n=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;i\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the integers \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\) are distinct, \\(|\\mathcal V'_n|=n-k\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V'_n\\) are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct from the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) (a sunny line is never vertical).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}\\cup\\mathcal V'_n .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=k+(n-k)=n\\) and \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)=P_j\\) for some \\(j\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\subset\\mathcal F_k\\)