"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \n\nFor an odd integer \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and for an even integer \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised.  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Maximum number of lattice points on a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has a direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with integers \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Between two consecutive lattice points on the line the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   If \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac n2\\).  \n   Thus \\(n\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(\\frac{n^2}{2}\\) points, whereas  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^2}{2}.\n   \\]  \n\n   Consequently \\(k=n\\) cannot be achieved when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n3. **Base configuration with no sunny lines.**  \n   For any integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the \\(m\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1,\\qquad x+y=m+1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\)) and together cover the whole set \\(S_m\\).\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Let \\(t\\) be the desired number of sunny lines, \\(0\\le t\\le n\\).*\n\n   - **Sunny part.**  \n     Use the \\(t\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=x+2,\\dots ,y=x+(t-1)\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     (equivalently \\(a-b=0,-1,\\dots ,-(t-1)\\)).  \n     They contain exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) satisfies \\(a-b\\ge -(t-1)\\).\n\n   - **Remaining points.**  \n     The points not covered by (3) satisfy \\(a-b\\le -t\\); write \\(b'=b-t\\).  \n     Then \\(a,b'>0\\) and  \n\n     \\[\n     a+b' = a+b-t\\le n+1-t,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. \\((a,b')\\in S_{\\,n-t}\\).  \n     By the base configuration (2) applied with \\(m=n-t\\) we can cover this remainder with exactly \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   - **Total.**  \n     The whole collection consists of \\(t+(n-t)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(t\\) are sunny.  \n     Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable *provided* the bound of step\u202f1 does not forbid it.\n\n5. **All\u2011sunny case for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).  \n   Consider the \\(n\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   y=x-d\\qquad (d=-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(d\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(2a+d\\le 2m+2\\), giving exactly \\(m+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integer solutions \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Different values of \\(d\\) give disjoint point sets, and the total number of points covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=|S_n|.\n   \\]  \n\n   Thus for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is also realizable.\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   - If \\(n\\) is odd, step\u202f5 shows \\(k=n\\) is possible, and step\u202f4 gives all smaller \\(k\\).  \n   - If \\(n\\) is even, step\u202f2 shows \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while step\u202f4 supplies constructions for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n\n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those stated in the boxed answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(p x+q y=r\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, \\(q\\neq0\\); not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, \\(p\\neq0\\); and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\), then \\((a'-a,b'-b)=(kp,kq)\\) for some non\u2011zero integer \\(k\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)=k(p+q).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p,q\\) are integers, \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the only possibilities \\(|p+q|=0,1\\) would force \\(p\\) or \\(q\\) to be \\(0\\) or \\(p=-q\\), contradicting the definition of sunny).  \nHence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) when moving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of such points on \\(\\ell\\) cannot exceed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Base configuration)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the \\(m\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1,\\qquad x+y=m+1\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_m\\) has \\(a\\le m-1\\) then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=m\\) then \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), and the point \\((m,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=m+1\\).  \nThus each point of \\(S_m\\) belongs to one of the lines in (7). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction with prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(0\\le t\\le n\\). There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(t\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(t\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\ny=x,\\;y=x+1,\\dots ,y=x+(t-1)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(i.e. the lines \\(a-b=0,-1,\\dots ,-(t-1)\\)).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on one of these lines iff \\(a-b\\ge -(t-1)\\).\n\nConsider the remaining points, i.e. those with \\(a-b\\le -t\\).  \nWrite \\(b'=b-t\\); then \\(b'>0\\) and\n\n\\[\na+b' = a+b-t\\le n+1-t .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b')\\in S_{\\,n-t}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 applied with \\(m=n-t\\) we can cover the set \\(S_{\\,n-t}\\) with exactly \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (vertical lines and one line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThese \\(n-t\\) lines are distinct from the sunny lines (their slopes are \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)), so altogether we have \\(t+(n-t)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(t\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (All\u2011sunny configuration for odd \\(n\\))\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\ny=x-d\\qquad (d=-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na+(a+d)\\le 2m+2\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; 1\\le a\\le m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the line contains exactly \\(m+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nDifferent values of \\(d\\) give different values of \\(b-a\\), so the point sets are disjoint.  \nConsequently the total number of covered points is  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nhence the \\(n\\) sunny lines (9) cover all of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\))\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, no configuration of \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even, Lemma\u202f1 gives the bound \\(M=\\frac n2\\) for the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that a single sunny line can contain.  \nThus \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot M = n\\cdot\\frac n2 = \\frac{n^{2}}2\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > \\frac{n^{2}}2\\).  \nHence covering all points with only sunny lines is impossible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nLemma\u202f4 provides a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 yields configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nThus all integers \\(k\\) in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are attainable.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nLemma\u202f5 shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  \nLemma\u202f3 supplies configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
