\(\\Sigma_j\\) is a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb R\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha_j\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_j\\notin\\Sigma_j\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(The set on the right‑hand side is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha_j\\) exists.)\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-b_j=\\alpha_j\\,(x-a_j).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Because of (1), \\(\\alpha_j\\neq0,-1\\); therefore \\(S_j\\) is sunny.*  \n*Because \\(\\alpha_j\\notin\\Sigma_j\\), the line \\(S_j\\) does **not** pass through any point of \\(T_n\\) other than \\(P_j\\).*  \nThus each \\(S_j\\) is a sunny line meeting \\(T_n\\) exactly at the chosen point \\(P_j\\).  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The final family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nRemove the hypotenuse \\(L_{n+1}\\) from the base family \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) and insert the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) lines; together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F_k|=(n-1)+k=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) is one of the selected points \\(P_j\\); by construction it lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\subset\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(L_s\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
