            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two extreme constructions\n\n*Zero sunny lines.*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*All lines sunny.*  \nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that partition \\(T_n\\).  \nWrite the points of \\(T_n\\) in rows according to their sum:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\text{sum }2:\\;(1,1)\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{sum }3:\\;(1,2),(2,1)\\\\[2pt]\n\\vdots\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{sum }n+1:\\;(1,n),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-(j)=\\alpha_j\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\n\\qquad\\alpha_j\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(\\alpha_j\\) are chosen inductively as follows.\nAssume \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_{j-1}\\) have already been fixed.\nConsider the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_j=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-a}{c-1}\\;\\Big|\\;(c,b)\\in T_n,\\;(c,b)\\neq(1,j)\\Bigr\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the slopes of all lines joining the point \\((1,j)\\) with any other point of \\(T_n\\).\nBecause \\(T_n\\) is finite, \\(\\Sigma_j\\) is finite.  \nChoose \\(\\alpha_j\\) so that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_j\\notin\\Sigma_j\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuch a choice is possible because the right‑hand side is a finite set.  \nBy (1) the line \\(S_j\\) is sunny and meets \\(T_n\\) **only** at the point \\((1,j)\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are distinct, hence the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\) are distinct.\n\nNow every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the diagonals \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) (these are already covered by the lines \\(S_j\\) because each such diagonal contains a point \\((1,s-1)\\) which is covered by \\(S_{s-1}\\)), or it is one of the points \\((1,j)\\) itself, which lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n=\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_n\\) and consists entirely of sunny lines; hence \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma – a non‑sunny line must be a diagonal\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is *not* sunny and that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> Then \\(\\ell\\) is one of the diagonals \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).\n\n*Horizontal.*  Write \\(\\ell:y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nIf a covering family of \\(n\\) lines contained a horizontal line, the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would have to cover the other \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n\\) points.  \nEach of those remaining lines can intersect a given horizontal line in at most one point, so each of them can contain at most one point from each diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) remaining lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)-(n)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n,\n\\]\n\nwhich equals the number of points still uncovered.  \nEquality would force every remaining line to be a diagonal, but then each diagonal would be disjoint from the horizontal line, contradicting the assumption that the horizontal line contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a horizontal line cannot appear in a covering family.\n\n*Vertical* is analogous (symmetry \\(x\\leftrightarrow y\\)) and leads to the same contradiction.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  Any line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=s\\) for some constant \\(s\\).  \nIf it contains a point of \\(T_n\\) then \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), i.e. the line is exactly the diagonal \\(D_s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting argument\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and that at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny.  \nLet \\(m\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (\\(1\\le m\\le n\\)).  \nBy the Lemma, those \\(m\\) lines are distinct diagonals, say \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_m}\\) with \\(2\\le s_1<\\dots <s_m\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the chosen diagonals are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m} D_{s_i},\n\\qquad\\text{and they are pairwise disjoint.}\n\\]\n\nHence the number of points already covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(s_i-1)\\le\\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-m\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.  \nA sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\); therefore it meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.  \nConsequently each sunny line can contribute at most one *new* point that is not already on one of the \\(m\\) chosen diagonals.  \nThus the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le\\frac{m(m+1)}2+(n-m).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nSubtracting the right‑hand side of (3) from \\(|T_n|\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl(\\frac{m(m+1)}2+(n-m)\\Bigr)\n   =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge m\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), the expression is non‑negative and equals zero **only when \\(m=n\\)**.  \nIf \\(m<n\\) the right‑hand side of (3) is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\); consequently the family would fail to cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nTherefore a covering family that contains any non‑sunny line must actually contain **all** \\(n\\) lines as diagonals, i.e. it contains **no** sunny line.  \n\nHence the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines are diagonals);  \n* \\(k=n\\) (no diagonal is used, all lines are sunny – the construction of §1 provides such a family).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
