id 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=\\alpha_i(x-1)+i,\n\\]\n\nwhere the real numbers \\(\\alpha_i\\) are chosen inductively so that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-a}{c-1}\\;\\Big|\\;(c,b)\\in T_n,\\;(c,b)\\neq(1,i)\\Bigr\\}\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe set on the right‑hand side of (1) is finite; therefore a suitable \\(\\alpha_i\\) exists.  \nBy construction \\((1,i)\\in S_i\\).  If \\((c,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((c,b)\\neq(1,i)\\) also lay on \\(S_i\\), then \\(\\alpha_i=\\dfrac{b-i}{c-1}\\), contradicting (1).  Hence each \\(S_i\\) meets \\(T_n\\) **only** at \\((1,i)\\).  The lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\) are distinct, all have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) (so they are sunny), and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Thus a covering with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A non‑sunny line must be a diagonal\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not sunny and that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  Then \\(\\ell\\) is one of the diagonals \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑sunny line has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal).\n\n*If the slope is \\(-1\\)*, the line is precisely \\(D_s\\) for some \\(s\\).\n\n*If the slope is \\(0\\)*, write \\(\\ell:y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains such a horizontal line.  \nEvery other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) can intersect \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point, so the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines together can contribute at most \\((n-1)n\\) new points.  Hence the whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)n=n^2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Therefore a horizontal line cannot belong to a covering family.  \n\n*The vertical case* is symmetric.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting the maximal number of points that can be covered\n\nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains exactly \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines (\\(0\\le m\\le n\\)).  \nBy Lemma 1 those \\(m\\) lines are distinct diagonals.  \nTo maximise the number of points they cover we may (and shall) take the \\(m\\) *largest* diagonals, namely  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1},\\;D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-m+2}.\n\\]\n\nThese diagonals are pairwise disjoint on \\(T_n\\); using (A) we obtain the total number of points already covered:\n\n\\[\nM(m)=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(n-j)=\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are sunny.  A sunny line can intersect each of the *remaining* diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_2,\\dots ,D_{n-m+1}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point, hence can contain at most \\(n-m\\) new points of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the \\(n-m\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-m)^2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nadditional points.\n\nTherefore any covering family with \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nM(m)+(n-m)^2\n   =\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}2+(n-m)^2\n   =\\frac{2n^2-2nm+m^2+m}{2}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (4) must be an equality.  Equating (4) with \\(|T_n|\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2n^2-2nm+m^2+m}{2}= \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n\\Longleftrightarrow (n-m)^2-(n-m)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nn-m\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(k=n-m\\) is the number of sunny lines.  The equality forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a covering family can contain **no** sunny line, **exactly one** sunny line, or **all** \\(n\\) li