\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), equality must hold in (3).  Simplifying the equality\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2n^{2}-2nm+m^{2}+m}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)^2-(n-m)=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n-m\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus either \\(m=n\\) (no sunny line) or \\(m=n-1\\) (exactly one sunny line).  The complementary description in terms of the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-m\\in\\{0,1,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   No other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The three values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are attainable by the explicit families displayed in §2, and the counting argument of §4 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Hence the answer is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n\\}} .\n   \\]"
