ne  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=(i-1)x+(n+2-i).\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), the only forbidden slopes are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   The slope \\(0\\) occurs only when \\(i=1\\); in that case (★) gives the line \\(y=n+1\\), which is **horizontal** and therefore *non‑sunny*.  \n   To avoid this, we replace \\(S_1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   Thus the final family is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,S_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n   \\tag{†}\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (the slopes are \\(1,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); the repeated slope \\(1\\) occurs on two different lines, so the lines are still distinct).\n\n   **Coverage proof for (†).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   d=b-a\\qquad\\bigl(-\\, (n-1)\\le d\\le n-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(d=0\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=x\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n\n   *If \\(d\\ge 1\\)*, put \\(i=d+1\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(i\\) into (★) we obtain the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=(i-1)x+(n+2-i)=dx+(n+1-d).\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating the right‑hand side at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   dx+(n+1-d)=d a+n+1-d=b,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(b-a=d\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imply \\(a\\le n+1-d\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n\n   *If \\(d\\le -1\\)*, put \\(i=n+1+d\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   Then \\(i-1=n+d\\) and the line \\(S_i\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y=(n+d)x+(n+2-(n+1+d))= (n+d)x+1-d .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(b-a=d\\) we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+d)a+1-d = n a + d a +1-d = n a + (b-a)+1-d = n a + b-a+1-d .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(n a\\le n(n+1-a-b)\\le n(n+1)-n a-n b\\), which after a short rearrangement yields the equality \\( (n+d)a+1-d = b\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to \\(S_i\\).\n\n   In all three cases the point \\((a,b