=0}^{m-1}(n-j)=\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   The remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are sunny.  A sunny line can intersect each of the *remaining* diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n-m+1}\n   \\]\n\n   in at most one point; therefore a single sunny line can contain at most \\(n-m\\) new points of \\(T_n\\).  With \\(n-m\\) sunny lines we can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)^2\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   additional points.\n\n   Consequently any covering family with \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M(m)+(n-m)^2\n   =\\frac{m(2n-m+1)}2+(n-m)^2\n   =\\frac{2n^{2}-2nm+m^{2}+m}{2}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), equality must hold in (3).  Simplifying the equality\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2n^{2}-2nm+m^{2}+m}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)^2-(n-m)=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n-m\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus either \\(m=n\\) (no sunny line) or \\(m=n-1\\) (exactly one sunny line).  The complementary description in terms of the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-m\\in\\{0,1,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   No other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The three values \\(k=0,1,n\\) are attainable by the explicit families displayed in §2, and the counting argument of §4 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Hence the answer is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
