ny line can cover at most two points, and with four lines we can cover at most eight points, which is insufficient.  Hence \\(m=4\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Existence for all other \\(m\\).**  \n   *Base cases.*  \n   - \\(m=0\\): the empty family of lines trivially covers the empty set.  \n   - \\(m=1\\): the single point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by any line through it whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   - \\(m=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y=x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y=-2x+5 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step for \\(m\\ge 5\\).*  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge 5\\) the set \\(T_{m-3}\\) can be covered by \\(m-3\\) sunny lines.  \n   Consider the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(2,2),\\qquad P_3=(3,3),\n   \\]\n\n   which all belong to \\(T_m\\) because \\(3+3\\le m+1\\).  \n   They lie on the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\; y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   Remove \\(L_0\\) and the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) from the picture.  \n   The remaining points are exactly the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1,\\; a\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m-1\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating the first part by the vector \\((-1,-1)\\) sends it bijectively onto \\(T_{m-3}\\).  \n   The two “border’’ families \\(\\{(a,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,b)\\}\\) are each a set of \\(m-1\\) points lying on the two distinct sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{m+1}{2},\\qquad\n   L_2:\\; y=-2x+m+2,\n   \\]\n\n   respectively; each of these lines contains exactly the points of one border family and no other point of \\(T_m\\).  \n   Consequently the whole set \\(T_m\\) is covered by the \\(m\\) sunny lines  